{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment:** `requirements.authoring.usage_inputs` is implemented completely and tested. I found no gaps and no acknowledged FIXMEs.\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Sizes and kinds.**\n  - `UsageLog::prepared` (src/authoring/usage.rs:142) pushes one `InputUsage` per input.\n  - Each record has an evidence `kind` (`outline`, `requirements`, `definition`, plus a `builtin` kind for fixed built-in text), UTF-8 `bytes` and `lines`, and an `origin`.\n  - Bytes are computed with `str::len` plus the joining newlines. Lines are the length of the `text` array.\n  - The `origin` is `\"mandatory\"` or the tool name (`habeas`, `ripgrep`), so mandatory and additional inputs are recorded the same way.\n  - Mandatory inputs are recorded in `assess_subject` and `followup_sources`. Tool-delivered inputs are recorded in `converse` (`usage.prepared(call…name, &delivery.inputs)`).\n- **Matched declarations.** `components` is the length of `matches` for a requirements input and 1 for other inputs.\n- **Unique versus repeated.** `unique` comes from a `seen` set keyed on the full serialized input. This also makes a repeat of a mandatory input by a later read non-unique.\n- **Successful versus failed reads.**\n  - `UsageLog::tool` increments `tool_calls` for every call and `tool_failures` for failed ones.\n  - `converse` reports a dispatch error as a failed call.\n  - Skipped calls are counted separately by `skipped_tool`.\n- **Prepared versus sent.**\n  - `begin_request` stamps `submitted_in_request` on inputs that were prepared before the request.\n  - `end_request` stamps `sent_in_request` only when the provider completed the request. A failed or retried request therefore leaves the input unsent.\n  - Inputs prepared after a response are submitted in the next request.\n- **File discovery.** `list_files` goes through `usage.tool(...)`, so it counts as a tool call. Its dispatch returns no inputs, so nothing is added to `inputs`.\n\nThe other fields and behaviours in the `{impl}` code (`origin`, `tool_name` sanitising, `skipped_tool`) are covered by sibling usage requirements marked on the same code, or are minimal support for this requirement. I found no unspecified behaviour.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source` (usage.rs) covers:\n  - UTF-8 byte counting (`λ`) and line counts;\n  - the matched-declaration count (2);\n  - the `outline` kind;\n  - a unique first read and a repeated `habeas` read (`unique: false`);\n  - the prepared → submitted → sent progression;\n  - that no source content is stored.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` covers a failed request leaving `sent_in_request` null until a retry succeeds.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` covers `submitted_in_request` 0 and `sent_in_request` 8 after eight failed attempts.\n- `tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text` covers tool calls, failures and skips as separate counts, and `list_files` and `outline` as tool calls with no inputs.\n- The CLI test `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` exercises the whole path end to end:\n  - a `list_files` call counts as a tool call and produces no input with origin `list_files`;\n  - `ripgrep` and `habeas` inputs have bytes and lines above zero;\n  - a repeated `habeas` read has `unique == false` and `sent_in_request == 1`;\n  - a mandatory `definition` input has non-zero bytes;\n  - a failed `habeas` read is counted in `tool_failures`.\n\n**Minor test gaps**\n\nThe `builtin` kind and the `components == 1` default for non-requirements inputs have no direct assertions. Both are trivial and do not affect the judgment.",
  "references": "dc43fb7d9d6f3d57673af4f00f38c1477c5deda399f79d1bccb7a3770e5c7190",
